Thermal discontinuity in pressure equilibrium (2D)
--------------------------------------------------

A cold, dense slab (density contrast 4, the middle half of a 2 x 1 periodic
box made of two copies of a 2D glass) in pressure equilibrium with the hot
gas around it, at rest. The particles keep the spacing of the glass and carry
the contrast in their masses; their internal energies are set from the SPH
density of the particle set (Wendland C4, eta 1.5), so that the pressure is
uniform at the start to the accuracy of the kernel-sum density.

The exact solution is static, so every change is numerical:

  - the growth of the width sigma of the u profile at the two interfaces
    (erf profiles, sigma^2 = sigma0^2 + 2 kappa_eff t) measures the
    artificial thermal conduction of the scheme at a contact,
  - the pressure blip at the interfaces (max |P / P0 - 1|) and the velocities
    it drives (max |v| / cs, rms v) measure what the conduction is there to
    remove: the spurious surface tension of the density estimate across an
    entropy jump,
  - the total energy must be conserved.

makeIC.py: --contrast, --ncopies, --eta, --nominal (u from the step density,
i.e. with the initial pressure blip of a naive set-up).

The code needs to be compiled with --with-hydro-dimension=2.
